SM(296,{"n":"Exodus 18","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Exodus 18.mp3","d":2685.6,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.36,"When we get started here in just a moment, we're going to pick up at the beginning of Exodus chapter 18."],[27.68,"So, by way of catch-up, uh last time we talked about, well, two times ago, we talked about how there were two problems."],[36.8,"We're sort of at the beginning, we're in between Israel having come through the Red Sea before they get to Mount Sinai."],[43.92,"We're probably probably tonight, no guarantees, gonna get to the point where they arrive at Mount Sinai."],[49.12,"But they're on the way."],[50.8,"Uh, but as we know, because the text itself has tipped us off, they're gonna end up, it's not just me spoiling, the text spoiled it itself."],[58.4,"They're gonna end up wandering in the Sinai Desert for 40 years."],[61.72,"But there are these two problems."],[63.32,"They didn't have food, they didn't have water."],[65.72,"So, two Bible studies ago, we talked about how God dealt with the food problem with manna and quail."],[73.16,"Last time we talked about how God dealt with the water problem with the water from the rock, and uh we talked about God coming to stand on the rock, uh, etc."],[85.16,"And then after taking care of the water issue, then we got our first major visit from the Amalekites."],[93.32,"As I mentioned, they'll be coming back around here for a while for several books until we get into 2 Samuel or 2nd Kingdoms."],[102.44,"I think the last time that's the last time they come around."],[103.72,"time they come around. But this was the first"],[107.28,"And so we talked about how God gave the Israelites victory, protection from the Amalekites when they descended upon them to try to wipe them out in the desert."],[120.48,"So that's where we ended last time."],[125.44,"And as we pick up, as I said, we're we're getting closer to Mount Sinai when the Torah is going to be officially given to Moses."],[137.28,"So, unless anybody has any comments or questions or stories of near-death experiences when they locked their knees, we'll go ahead and uh pick up in Exodus chapter 18. verse 1"],[152.12,"Now Jethro, the priest of Midian, Moses' father-in-law, heard about all God did for Moses and for Israel his people, that the Lord brought Israel out of Egypt."],[162.12,"Okay, so Jethro's coming back by."],[166.6,"You met Jethro before."],[168.52,"Jethro's Moses' father-in-law, his wife Zipporah is his daughter."],[173.48,"And remember, during the 40 years after Moses fled from Egypt, before he went back to Egypt to get the people, for that 40 years, he was living with Jethro as part of Jethro's household."],[188.52,"He was married to his daughter."],[190.84,"He was herding Jethro's her.ds and flocks"],[196.62,"And that's what he was doing, remember, when he came upon the burning bush and got sent in commission."],[202.7,"So now Jethro, we talked about how Jethro is actually a title, not his proper given name."],[210.3,"It seems to be a title among the Midianites for uh a priest."],[216.3,"Um but so he's now heard about what happened after Moses departed and went back to Egypt."],[223.82,"He's heard about the defeat of Hara's army, he's heard about the plagues, he's heard about the crossing of the Red Sea."],[229.5,"And remember, uh, as we were going through that story, I know it's a while ago for the people sitting here, less so for the people hearing this recording."],[238.3,"But uh and over again, God mentioned this in terms of the plagues and in terms of what he was doing in the Red Sea."],[247.54,"That part of why he was doing it was so that his name would be made known among the nations, the other nations of the world."],[258.18,"Uh that he had sort of three levels."],[261.08,"He's revealing himself to Israel."],[263.57,"But that it was very clearly not a case where it's like, well, God cares about Israel and he doesn't care about any of these other people."],[269.67,"It was God was revealing himself to Israel."],[273.11,"Through what he was doing with Israel, he was revealing himself to the Egyptians, and then through what he was doing with Israel and with Egypt, he was revealing himself. to all the nations of the world"],[286.83,"So this is the first sign we get here, that Jethro out in the desert, who's living as a nomad on the desert, has heard about what happened."],[295.89,"We're going to continue to get signs of this, even into the book of Joshua and later on, where people they encounter will have heard about what happened, with Egypt."],[306.95,"And that will have been given a certain reputation to the God of Israel, when they show up."],[315.13,"But for now, his father-in-law is coming for a visit"],[319.53,"Verse 2. Then Jethro, Moses' father-in-law, took Zipporah, Moses' wife, after he sent her back with her two sons, of whom the name of one was Gershem, for he said, I was a sojourner in a foreign land, and the name of the other was Eliezer, for he said, The God of my father is my help, and he rescued me from the hand of Pharaoh."],[341.17,"And Jethro, Moses' father in law, came with his sons and his wife to Moses in the desert, where he encamped at the mountain of God."],[359.17,"As was God, uh, and she had had to take care of circumcising."],[365.22,"There's not because Moses hadn't."],[368.68,"But so he hadn't taken them with him."],[370.44,"So now J.ethro is bringing them to him"],[374.48,"Um, we get they sort of repeat the origin of their names."],[378.5,"Gersham, Gur means foreigner, stranger, sojourner."],[383.54,"Um, so he named his first son Gersham, because at that time he was sojourning out in the desert."],[389.96,"Living with Jethro."],[391.64,"And the second son is Eliezer, which means God is my help, as it says there, because he said, The God of my fathers is my help, and he rescued me from the hand of Pharaoh."],[408.12,"Verse 6. Now he had said to Moses, I, your father-in-law Jethro, Jethro, am coming to you with your wife and two sons with her."],[414.68,"So he's not showing up unannounced."],[415.32,"So Moses went out to meet his father-in-law, bowed down and kissed him"],[422.0,"Thus they greeted one another, and he brought them into the tent."],[426.0,"Then Moses told his father-in-law all the Lord had done to Pharaoh and to all the Egyptians for Israel's sake, and all the hardship that had come upon them on the way, and how the Lord had delivered them out of the hand of Pharaoh and from the hand of the Egyptians."],[445.02,"Heard rumors, but Moses kind of fills him in on the whole story of everything that happened."],[451.9,"Then Jethro was amazed for all the good things the Lord had done for Israel, whom he delivered out of the hand of Pharaoh and from the hand of the Egyptians."],[459.1,"Then Jethro said, Blessed be the Lord who delivered you out of the hand of Pharaoh and from the hand of the Egyptians."],[466.74,"Now I know the Lord is great above all the gods."],[469.62,"For this reason they did not prevail over them."],[475.86,"Remember, uh Lord here, is actually covering for the name Yahweh."],[485.63,"And we talked about the fact that the earliest record outside of the Torah."],[493.65,"Outside of the Old Testament, the Hebrew Bible, of the name Yahweh for a god being used by anybody, uh comes from a couple of Egyptian inscriptions. from roughly this period"],[508.5,"And in that period, they refer to Yahweh as the god of the Shasu Bedouins."],[520.5,"And we say, okay, who's that?"],[522.5,"That's the Midianites."],[524.58,"That's the Edomites and the Midianites who lived nomadically in the desert in the Sinai Peninsula."],[532.5,"So that's our earliest archaeological evidence. for the name Yahweh is that this is the God that was worshipped by people like Jethro"],[540.84,"We commented on that a little bit back when we first met Jethro because he's out doing these sacrifices and stuff that Moses is participating in."],[548.7,"We're talking about how this isn't."],[552.47,"He's not like a pagan priest, to like this is the same God."],[556.63,"Because the Midianites are made up primarily of Ishmaelites and Edomites."],[563.75,"So these are descended from Ishmael and Esau."],[565.99,"So these are people descended from Abraham."],[568.79,"These are the Israelites' cousins, basically."],[571.99,"These are people descended from Abraham."],[574.23,"They're still worshiping Abraham's God at this point, and living nomadically the way that Abraham lived."],[582.73,"And so that's why this is this is the same, this is the same God."],[588.19,"But remember, we talked about it the Passover when God announced what he was about to do in the actual Passover event in the death of the firstborn in Egypt."],[598.76,"God said that that night he would judge all the gods of Egypt."],[603.86,"And we commented at the time, God wasn't saying, I am going to judge a bunch of fictional characters."],[611.83,"Because that's not a display of your might and power."],[616.29,"If Batman were real, beating up Batman would mean that you were really tough."],[621.57,"But since he's not, you being able to beat Batman, who's a fictional character, is not impressive."],[629.67,"So when God says he's going to display his power by defeating these gods.s, he' not talking about things that don't exist"],[636.97,"So the perspective we get here, and it's reflected here too, is that those Egyptian gods existed."],[644.03,"There were actual spirits who they were out there worshiping and interacting with and doing stuff with."],[651.19,"But God rendered judgment on them."],[653.59,"Why? Because how had they led the Egyptians?"],[660.87,"Slavery, oppression, military conquest."],[665.77,"What was their culture, their society like?"],[668.73,"Brutal, repressive, violent."],[672.95,"So these were not spirits that had their best interests at heart."],[677.97,"They were not there to help humans flourish, and live abundant, healthy lives."],[684.76,"These are spirits with other motives."],[687.58,"And so God judges them, and by judging them, as we talked about in the plagues, Yahweh demonstrates that there is this category difference between him and these other gods."],[701.18,"That category difference is not exists versus doesn't exist."],[706.28,"That category difference is creator versus created spirits."],[712.66,"Creator of everything versus"],[715.28,"ir Spits that were created and came into being at some point."],[719.73,"That's the big major category difference."],[723.89,"And so the word here in the Greek that's translated as now I know."],[731.11,"It may sound like, oh, he didn't know that before."],[734.79,"He was already worshipping Yahweh."],[737.93,"But the Greek word there is actually a word that is used to know, but it's built off of a verb to see."],[749.9,"So now I know."],[751.74,"I mean, while it's a legit translation, it's more like, oh, I see."],[758.08,"Like if someone demonstrates something to you, someone went on a chalkboard and said, Let me show you how this works out."],[764.01,"And worked it out."],[765.39,"When they were done, you would know something."],[768.41,"But it would be like, okay, now I see it."],[770.49,"Now I get it."],[771.55,"Now this is plain to me."],[774.73,"So that's the idea of what he's saying."],[776.49,"He's saying, what just happened, this shows, this demonstrates, this, you know, reveals to me."],[783.37,"Now I know that Yahweh is in this other category, different than and above these other gods, like the Egyptian gods."],[793.93,"And that that's and that's demonstrated because when he came to judge them, there was nothing they could do."],[798.81,"there was nothing they could do. When he came and turned the Nile to blood"],[806.58,"When he set darkness upon the land, Ray, the sun god."],[812.95,"Could not oppose him, could not prevail against him."],[816.95,"Verse 12. That Jethro, Moses' father-in-law, took whole burnt offerings and sacrifices for God."],[824.15,"For Aaron came with all the elders of Israel to eat bread with Moses' father in law before God."],[831.11,"Okay, so they come in and offer sacrifices."],[836.15,"Note again, we'll get into this a lot more when we get into Leviticus and we're talking about the specific sacrifices, which won't be as long as you think."],[844.23,"But notice how this is described"],[844.43,"It's the next book after Exodus."],[850.27,"It says they're offering sacrifices, and then it says they ate bread before the Lord."],[858.19,"This is an important thing."],[860.03,"So I'm just I'm planting the seed now."],[863.71,"Sacrifices are not about killing things, they're about eating things."],[876.67,"Well, I could kill a basket of breadsticks at Olive Garden, but that's a an analogical use of the word kill."],[887.47,"You're not actually right stabbing, there's no blood coming out of the bread."],[893.59,"So does that mean when they were doing the Moloch stuff, it's not like you see on TV where they just throw the baby into the thing?"],[899.27,"No, there was can be human sacrifices involved cannibalism, yes."],[903.43,"Oh no. Of various kinds."],[905.11,"Yes. Often blood drinking, but sometimes more than that."],[909.03,"This is a thank offering for the exes?"],[912.47,"Just in general, for all of for all of those things."],[915.29,"So there's whole burnt offerings where you burn the whole thing, and then there's sacrifices where the choice portions we talked about this a little with the sacrifices with uh, but the choice pieces go to God, and then the the there are other parts that are eaten by the people. making the offering"],[936.05,"And so um uh sacrifice exists, and again, we'll get into more detail in Leviticus, but sacrifice exists within the broader idea of hospitality."],[953.62,"Hospitality, which is a building of community."],[958.98,"And so communities in the ancient world included the gods, god or gods of those communities."],[967.3,"And so sacrifice and the offering of incense and the offering of other gifts, is all within the context of hospitality, reaffirming the relationship between the people and each other and the people in God, or restoring it if that's been broken."],[986.97,"The relationships between people and their relationships with the divine."],[991.55,"That's what sacrifice is always doing."],[994.11,"It involves eating, it involves this meal that's eaten together."],[1000.27,"So it seems like Jethro's kind of taking the lead on the whole burnt offerings."],[1005.15,"Yes. Is that just like Moses being humble and deferring to his father in law, or is this like Jethro doing this to to be honor Yahweh because he wasn't present for that?"],[1017.99,"Or so at this point, at this point we're at right now. there is no separate priesthood yet"],[1028.79,"Right now, Aaron's mentioned there prominently."],[1031.53,"Yeah, and that's kind of a looking forward right to what's gonna happen."],[1036.49,"We talked about when we talked about that incident with the circumcision of his son, about how that's where some of Moses' authority gets taken away from him and given to Aaron."],[1048.99,"In that whole interchange between him and God, and then um and as that turns out, that's gonna end up being priestly authority."],[1057.31,"But that sort of separation hasn't fully happened yet where we are right now."],[1060.91,"We're sort of on the cusp of it."],[1062.51,"So previous to this, what you see, and like what we saw with Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, is that it's the head of the household who is the priest for the household."],[1077.51,"And since Moses is part of Jethro's household."],[1079.83,"Moses is still part of Jethro's household."],[1082.55,"He's his father, essentially."],[1084.97,"And so it's the head of the household's duty to go and offer sacrifices."],[1088.89,"And this is what we see like in Job."],[1090.41,"Job is out making sacrifices for his children."],[1093.7,"And their families."],[1095.14,"So, and this is important because this is part of in the Torah, there's this integral link between what it means to be a man, what we would now call masculinity, and priesthood."],[1111.08,"And that's a relationship essentially of identity in the Torah."],[1117.82,"What it means to be a man is to be a priest."],[1121.9,"That's why you while you have you have female prophets, you have queens who rule in the Old Testament."],[1133.02,"The idea of a female priest is like a square circle."],[1138.22,"As far as the Old Testament is concerned."],[1140.14,"As far as the Hebrew Bible is concerned, and the Torah is concerned."],[1142.7,"That's an idea that literally doesn't make sense."],[1145.34,"It's like a married bachelor."],[1146.8,"You're like, that's wait, what?"],[1149.68,"You're saying it's a man woman?"],[1151.2,"What? Like, that doesn't make sense."],[1154.78,"So and that's not just in a negative sense."],[1159.03,"That's important to remember."],[1161.05,"Even even I was interviewed not too long ago about this, and someone asked, you know, why are women excluded from the priesthood?"],[1167.93,"And it's like that that doesn't make sense."],[1172.83,"That's the wrong way of even asking the question."],[1176.37,"That's taking priesthood as this thing out there to which some people are admitted and some people are not."],[1182.61,"And one of the things that disqualifies you is being female."],[1185.23,"That's not understanding priesthood as what it means to be male."],[1190.51,"And the reason I emphasize this now is not because, like, oh, we need to stop there from being woman priests of the Orthodox Church."],[1196.75,"s. Because there' literally not levers to make that happen"],[1198.23,"I literally don't worry about that."],[1203.99,"So I don't that doesn't keep me up at night."],[1206.31,"The reason I emphasize it now is all of this men's movement manosphere stuff on the internet."],[1214.28,"Which is telling you everything about what it means to be a man except being a priest."],[1221.85,"You say that I'm assuming you're not talking about like what you do."],[1225.61,"I'm talking about like liver king."],[1229.37,"I mean, in the sense of priest."],[1230.93,"Like the role of priest of like being a father."],[1233.37,"Well, analogically, yes."],[1235.77,"Analogically, yes. So it would be like being the spiritual leader of your family."],[1239.99,"Yeah So, yes."],[1243.83,"What you get online is you need to be like ripped."],[1247.59,"Ripped, rich, and uh sexually promiscuous."],[1253.24,"Which is kind of the opposite of being a priest."],[1256.3,"Like, I only have like two Bugattis."],[1258.78,"No. Um, they're like this, but they're Hot Wheels, but um."],[1266.3,"Um Yeah, kind of the opposite."],[1270.12,"But I mean, practically, this transforms a lot of things."],[1275.34,"Not just in terms of role within a family, but I mean, when you think about like disciplining children, that looks very different if you think about it as what happens when you go to confession with a priest, as opposed to like a contest of wills."],[1294.18,"I told you to do this and you didn't do it."],[1296.2,"And I'm going to show you that I'm the one in charge."],[1298.89,"I wouldn't get very far if that's how I approached things."],[1302.59,"In the church."],[1303.31,"If I was like yelling at people and like scolding them and trying to give them capital punishment, or capital corporal punishment."],[1310.29,"Like I wouldn't get very far."],[1314.02,"If you approach it as, you know, someone in my family has done something that separated them from God."],[1321.78,"And separated them from the rest of the family by their actions and the choices they've made."],[1325.14,"And my job is to reconcile them with the rest of the family and with God"],[1330.22,"It's a very different approach to dealing with things."],[1335.76,"And that gives us some of the meaning of like what Christ means when he said says, blessed are the peacemakers."],[1343.41,"Not blessed are the victorious, which is what the Romans thought."],[1346.71,"The ones who win."],[1348.63,"Christ says, Blessed are the ones who make peace and restore those things."],[1354.95,"So, yeah, so that's what's meant, sort of by priesthood."],[1359.59,"But so right now it is still lodged with, and we're going to see there are going to be concrete things that happen where certain aspects of that priesthood are taken away from some men because of their failures."],[1375.97,"But that doesn't, that doesn't change."],[1379.67,"The calling in the broader sense to all men."],[1384.47,"And even if you're not married, even if you're not, like in a family situation."],[1389.86,"The idea that within your social circle, within your friend group, within your place of employment, God is calling you to represent God to those people and to represent those people before God in prayer."],[1404.06,"That's a very, again, different idea of what it means to be a man than what you get from YouTube."],[1410.86,"I was supposed to make peace at work and not gossip"],[1417.28,"Yeah, for example."],[1418.56,"That's right. Yeah."],[1421.36,"Yeah. Um, so yeah."],[1425.92,"We'll come back to that again when some unfortunate incidents happen."],[1430.08,"So we start seeing the priesthood that being separated out."],[1434.56,"But it's good to have that broad idea in mind that that's sort of what masculinity should be."],[1442.61,"Ideally. Verse 13."],[1447.17,"So it was on the next day that Moses sat to judge the people, and the people stood before Moses from morning until evening."],[1453.73,"ing So it says judge the people, that doesn't mean he's just stand there going like Simon Cow."],[1453.89,"Get out of here"],[1460.81,"Look at this guy."],[1461.9,"It's not like that kind of judging."],[1464.14,"It's he's settling disputes."],[1467.48,"We got this camp full of people."],[1469.4,"They're out in the desert."],[1470.6,"We've already seen what they're like."],[1472.38,"With all the grumbling and the complaining and the you brought us out here to die, and we had it better off at eating."],[1477.6,"So as you can imagine, they're all living together."],[1479.68,"They don't all get along swimmingly all the time."],[1482.64,"And they have disputes about things."],[1484.26,"Like, my goat died, I think he poisoned it."],[1487.14,"Or or, you know, he stole my goat."],[1488.58,"That's not his goat, that's my goat."],[1490.35,"And on and on and on and on and on and on and on."],[1493.17,"Um, or he promised he'd pay me this and he didn't pay me me, or he owes me this, she owes that."],[1496.01,"So there are all these sort of constant petty disputes"],[1496.33,"he So when it says's judging the people, this means Moses says, Okay, I'll c I'll hear your complaints, and Moses is the one making the calls, like okay, you're right, okay, give him back his goat."],[1513.56,"Whatever. He's my friend."],[1515.64,"Um, yeah, so he's he's settling all of these things."],[1518.78,"Settling all of these disputes."],[1520.78,"And he literally spends the whole day doing it."],[1524.07,"From sunrise to sunset, he's hearing all of these complaints."],[1529.01,"They're trying to resolve the issues."],[1531.65,"So when Moses' father-in-law saw all he did for the people, he said, What is this thing you are doing for the people?"],[1537.57,"Why do you alone sit and all the people? stand before you from morning until evening"],[1542.81,"Then Moses said to his father-in-law, because the people come to me to seek judgment from God."],[1548.41,"When they have a dispute, they come to me, and I judge between them and another, and I make known the ordinances of God and his laws."],[1556.81,"So notice again, we've talked a few times as we've been going through here, the authority that's God's speaking."],[1565.93,"This is the opposite"],[1572.63,"God has given Moses this authority."],[1575.11,"So that when he resolves it, that's God's decision on the subject."],[1581.82,"That's God's decision on the subject."],[1583.1,"Um the authority Moses has"],[1587.22,"And so Moses explained to Jethro, well, they're coming to me because, you know, they go to somebody else."],[1592.42,"It's not the same authority."],[1596.07,"God has given me this authority, so I'm the one who has to make these calls."],[1601.09,"We talked before that."],[1602.21,"That this is the descriptions of Moses' authority that you get in pre Christian Judaism is where the terms binding and loosing that show up in the New Testament come from."],[1615.41,"The authority that Christ gives to his apostles."],[1618.87,"He's using the language that refers back to how they understood the authority given to Moses."],[1626.23,"In those particular issues"],[1627.31,"That he had this authority to make those calls."],[1634.81,"Even once we get to the end of the Torah, and there's all the commandments laid out, there's still going to be a thousand situations that arise where people will have questions."],[1647.77,"How exactly does this apply?"],[1649.93,"Does this one apply or does that one apply?"],[1652.75,"Well, my situation's a little different."],[1655.39,"And someone's gonna have to use it."],[1658.07,"Because we mentioned before, it's actually not possible for a text to exercise authority by itself."],[1665.71,"This may horrify some people listening to this recording later."],[1670.59,"But it's not possible for a text, even the Bible, even the scriptures, to have authority by itself."],[1677.67,"Because it's a book, it's a text."],[1680.47,"Someone has to read it and interpret it and apply it."],[1687.47,"And someone has to have the authority to do that."],[1693.71,"And that authority has to come from God because if it's just, well, I'm a smart dude."],[1699.77,"There are other smart dudes who will have different opinions."],[1707.9,"So it has to be someone who God has designated."],[1710.22,"So we're seeing here it's Moses."],[1714.64,"But they're going to be successors to Moses."],[1719.2,"All the way up to the time of Jesus."],[1720.72,"Because what does Jesus say about the Pharisees?"],[1723.28,"He says, do whatever the Pharisees say to do, because they sit in Moses' seat."],[1730.32,"They have that authority to interpret the laws and the ordinances, like Moses says here."],[1738.72,"Now Jesus then says, don't do what they do because they're hypocrites."],[1742.08,"But what they say."],[1744.29,"You should do."],[1745.17,"They should also do what they say."],[1748.61,"But. And so then when he gives his apostles that authority to bind and to loose."],[1756.24,"They become the ones to make those decisions and those calls."],[1763.24,"To the point that when St."],[1765.56,"Paul finds out that some of the people at his churches are taking disputes into Roman courts."],[1775.5,"He chews them out about it."],[1779.18,"He chews them out about it."],[1781.26,"He says, why are you going to pagans to resolve disputes with your fellow Christians?"],[1787.36,"I get it if you have a dispute with a pagan."],[1789.94,"But you have a dispute with your fellow Christians."],[1791.74,"What? Why are you going there to have it settled by Roman law?"],[1799.39,"And that' thats' thats still's in the canons of the church."],[1800.03,"'s Im not going to comment on any recent event."],[1803.83,"That's still to this day."],[1807.83,"But it is still the case that no matter who you are in the Orthodox Church, if you sue a fellow Orthodox Christian, you are done."],[1818.23,"You are laycized if you're clergy."],[1820.79,"You are excommunicated if you're not."],[1832.11,"Their job is to resolve these things."],[1835.59,"Resolve these disputes."],[1839.29,"And so if there is ever an issue between you and a fellow Christian."],[1844.83,"Christ talks about this in Matthew 18 and lays this out."],[1848.75,"But he's laying out a process that already really existed."],[1852.21,"Just reaffirming it."],[1853.83,"For the church."],[1854.79,"If you have a problem with someone, you talk to that person."],[1858.4,"And you try to resolve it together."],[1860.32,"If you can't resolve it together, you bring a couple more of your fellow Christians, and you all get together and put your heads together and you try and resolve whatever the issue is."],[1868.32,"If that doesn't work, you go to the priest."],[1873.27,"If the priest is unable to resolve it, or if the priest is one of the people who you're bad at, then the bishop resolves it."],[1879.9,"If you can't resolve it with the priest."],[1881.75,"Then you resolve it with the bishop."],[1883.35,"But then when you get to the bishop, the bishop's word is it."],[1888.45,"There's no place to go there."],[1889.57,"There has to be a place where it stops."],[1892.47,"There has to be a court of last appeal where what the bishop says, and that's not that bishop could ever be quote unquote wrong."],[1902.41,"It's not about wrong and right."],[1905.85,"So for us, I know for some other folks you have the concept of canon law."],[1910.17,"We don't. We have canons that are tools for the bishop to apply."],[1917.55,"And so, whatever the bishop's resolution is, that is the right thing for you and the other person in that situation."],[1927.07,"The right thing to do to is do. is for you to be obedient"],[1930.95,"Now, if the bishop is being ignorant, sloppy, vindictive."],[1937.84,"Sinful in some other way, that's something God will take up with that bishop on the day of judgment."],[1944.98,"That he's going to answer for."],[1947.67,"Or if I do the same thing, you come to me to try to resolve something, and I do that, that's something God will address with me on the day of judgment."],[1954.79,"That is a very serious thing."],[1958.28,"But each of our jobs, is to do our best to make peace and resolve issues, and then ultimately to be obedient to the authority that God has put over us."],[1968.34,"us. And when we do that, when we do that, then we're doing what God has asked of"],[1976.63,"And if that puts us in a position where we're suffering, where we're being mistreated, again, that is something God will resolve."],[1985.43,"God has said he's going to reward those who patiently suffer being mistreated."],[1990.41,"And he's going to deal with those who mistreat others."],[1995.37,"So we have to remember we't think somebody does horrible things and they die, they got away with it."],[2007.39,"God is going to deal with these things."],[2009.09,"Or if we get mistreated and we die, well, we just had a lousy life."],[2012.53,"That's not what we believe."],[2015.75,"God deals with all these things."],[2018.47,"But so there's always been this authority."],[2021.76,"Since the giving of the Torah, there has been a text that is used as a tool that is applied, that is interpreted by that authority."],[2031.6,"But there is always a human authority alongside it who is doing that interpreting and that applying, who has authority given to them from God to do it."],[2040.0,"But that doesn't mean my neighborly calls himself a Christian."],[2044.0,"What do you mean?"],[2044.72,"In terms of can you sue him?"],[2046.36,"I would I would I would go to pagan court with him."],[2049.6,"Well, you'd probably just practically you'd probably have to."],[2054.0,"Because he wouldn't the agree to bis bishohopp to your settling it, I don't imagine."],[2057.28,"If he did, great"],[2063.54,"If he said, okay, we could have your bishop solve it, that'd be great."],[2067.46,"That'd be ideal."],[2068.26,"Save you a lot of court fees."],[2072.42,"Yeah, go to both."],[2073.46,"Yeah. I mean, but one of us as a practical matter, you know, if that person won't."],[2080.98,"Yeah, and it's not like the bishop can excommunicate him."],[2085.54,"Because he's already not a member of the church."],[2088.6,"So you can't make him even more not a member of the church."],[2092.44,"Yeah. Can you appeal to other bishops?"],[2095.56,"No. You have you have a bishop."],[2098.84,"Oh, they can't call."],[2100.04,"Oh, okay Yeah, yeah, yeah."],[2101.32,"ah Um, so that's, ye."],[2101.32,"Ultimately, that's the place where it stops"],[2108.08,"In the Orthodox Church."],[2109.52,"Yeah. Verse 17."],[2113.68,"So Moses' father-in-law said to him, The thing you are doing is not good."],[2117.76,"That's a great roundabout translation."],[2122.56,"Both you and these people with you will surely wear yourselves out, for this thing is too much for you."],[2127.84,"You are not able to perform it by yourself."],[2131.06,"So he says to Moses, look, like you've got thousands of people in this camp."],[2138.16,"You can't do this every day."],[2139.84,"You won't be able to do er anything else."],[2143.05,"You need help."],[2144.17,"Verse 19. Now listen to me, I will give you counsel, and God will be with you"],[2148.45,"Stand before God for the people that you may bring their concerns to God."],[2152.69,"Teach them God's ordinances and laws and show them the way in which they must walk and the works they are to do."],[2159.89,"Okay. So he says, here's your job."],[2164.18,"Your job is this mediatorial role to pray for the people and represent them before God."],[2170.26,"And then the second piece is you go and represent God to the people."],[2174.74,"You teach them God's ways, his teachings, his laws, his ordinances, how to live."],[2179.92,"So you teach them God's teaching, you represent them before God."],[2184.96,"This is your job."],[2186.88,"This is what you need to do, Moses."],[2188.64,"Verse 21 Moreover, you shall select from all the people able men, such as fear God, just men, hating covetousness, and set them over the people to be rulers of thousands, hundreds, fifties, and tens."],[2208.28,"Let them judge the people at all times, then every great matter they shall bring to you, but every small matter they themselves shall judge."],[2217.08,"So it'll be easier for you, for they will relieve and help you."],[2221.88,"Okay So he's saying, You need some lesser clergy, Moses."],[2228.92,"It can't be just you."],[2231.53,"So find people. with these characteristics"],[2234.69,"Did you recognize any of those characteristics?"],[2236.61,"I know it's been a couple years since we went through 1st and 2 Timothy and Titus."],[2240.93,"Some of you weren't here."],[2243.97,"These are the same things that St."],[2247.09,"Paul references when he's talking about who should be made a presbyter, who should be made a deacon."],[2253.19,"In the church later on."],[2255.44,"Find men like this, he's saying to Timothy and Titus."],[2259.04,"What were Timothy and Titus?"],[2262.0,"Bishops. Find men like this, make them presbyters, make them deacons."],[2268.55,"Say Paul's not making this up."],[2272.47,"He's not just coming up with something."],[2274.63,"Oh, we got this. new religion"],[2277.31,"How are we going to do this?"],[2278.51,"Who's going to be in charge?"],[2281.73,"He's going back to the Torah."],[2283.65,"Same kind of structure."],[2284.77,"Moses here, sort of our paradigm of what a bishop is and what a bishop does."],[2290.11,"And then he's got other people helping him."],[2294.6,"And the great matters, the serious matters, the big issues, they bring to him."],[2299.4,"And the small issues they deal with themselves."],[2302.76,"So that he can continue in his primary job description, which is the same as the primary job description of a bishop."],[2311.64,"So that's where this comes from."],[2313.8,"This is also why when you when you see people who follow our 19th century German friends who want to say, like, oh, things like 1st and 2 Timothy and Titus, these had to be, these can't have been written by St."],[2328.4,"Paul. Because they didn't have all these complicated leadership structures in the church until like the second century, until like much later."],[2338.34,"You could put out how ridiculous that is because these structures were around for like 1500 years before St."],[2347.7,"Paul. Christianity was not a new religion."],[2354.82,"It was essentially a sect of Judaism."],[2358.5,"It was just continuing the Jewish tradition with the belief that the Jewish Messiah had come."],[2363.38,"Which, by the way, was a Jewish belief"],[2368.6,"Like, so there is no discontinuity there."],[2371.56,"The apostles weren't starting over from scratch."],[2374.2,"They have the same leadership structures that Jewish communities did."],[2379.5,"That are present all the way here from the Torah."],[2381.9,"That they just kept using."],[2383.5,"They didn't need time to develop them."],[2385.98,"They didn't start out with no leadership structures."],[2391.56,"There's never been a sorry, anarchists, but there has never been a culture, society, or even family in the history of the world that was actual anarchy."],[2404.04,"Where there was literally nobody in charge."],[2405.96,"It might just be the largest human."],[2409.69,"Who is the largest and the strongest and pushes everybody around?"],[2413.61,"It might be the elder member who's respected by people."],[2417.69,"It might be the smartest guy who manipulates everybody, but there's always somebody in charge."],[2425.1,"That includes in the early church there were people in charge."],[2429.63,"This idea that there was some utopian anarchist, com anarcho communist commune in the early church is falter all."],[2441.84,"Verse 23, if you do this thing, God will strengthen you."],[2445.04,"Then you will be able to endure, and all this people will also go to their place in peace."],[2449.04,"So Moses heeded the voice of his father-in-law and did all he said"],[2455.32,"Thus Moses chose able men out of all Israel and made them heads over the people, rulers of thousands, hundreds, fifties, and tens."],[2463.0,"They judged the people at all times, all the hard cases they brought to Moses, but the small ones they judged themselves."],[2469.64,"Then Moses let his father-in-law depart, and he returned to his own land."],[2473.72,"He being his father in law, Jethro."],[2475.56,"Went back to his own land."],[2484.42,"Not just that he let Jethro offer the sacrifices, but also here, he's willing to take advice."],[2492.63,"This doesn't mean we we saw his self-causing, he explained to Jethro."],[2496.55,"I'm the person God gave the authority to do this."],[2499.19,"So it's not that like he's not clear on that."],[2502.73,"He's totally clear on the authority God has given him."],[2505.45,"But he's still willing to accede to someone else's wise advice and listen, and take that counsel."],[2515.35,"Now later on, uh we're gonna be told in the Torah that Moses was the most humble man on earth to ever live."],[2522.07,"And of course, traditionally that was written by Moses."],[2524.63,"So you can figure that out on your own."],[2528.23,"But no, we'll get there, we'll talk about it."],[2531.03,"But um for the record, uh just to make it clear, every once in a while, people on the internet, you know, go, oh, Father Stephen's just making this stuff up."],[2545.73,"Because I don't like proof text church fathers when I go through this stuff."],[2549.33,"But let me point out that me understanding this is referring to bishops and priests and deacons."],[2556.05,"We read this scripture reading every time we have a feast of the ecumenical councils about the bishops."],[2563.97,"So, not making this up."],[2569.65,"Or plagiarizing. You can look at it that way."],[2571.81,"I'm plagiarizing uh the liturgical tradition of the church."],[2576.01,"And presenting it like I'm just smart."],[2578.29,"But yes, so there there are these structures put in place."],[2581.37,"Another parallel in the New Testament that you might think of is remember in the book of Acts."],[2590.09,"In the community in Jerusalem, you have the apostles, who are the ones in charge."],[2596.76,"And this issue comes up where someone comes and complains, well, some of the Greek speaking Jewish widows aren't getting fed and taken care of the way the Jewish widows are, and come to them with these practical problems."],[2612.36,"And what do the apostles say?"],[2616.16,"Deacons. We have this job that we've been given by God that we need to keep to."],[2622.16,"We can't handle all of these things."],[2624.48,"And so they go and appoint the first seven deacons."],[2630.1,"To take care of this issue so that they can keep doing their job."],[2633.54,"Again, this isn't them like shirking their duties or something."],[2636.9,"This isn't them inventing some new structure."],[2640.52,"This is they're going but go back to the Torah."],[2643.08,"Okay, how do we handle things like this?"],[2645.16,"Well, here's how Moses handled it."],[2649.62,"That's so this is the right thing to do."],[2652.12,"And when you read about the deacons, it describes the type of men they were, and you'll notice it's suspiciously similar to what we just read in Exodus."],[2663.24,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2668.36,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com"],[2675.84,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.36,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: food, water, Amalek","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Between <b>Red Sea</b> and <b>Mount Sinai</b> - maybe reach Sinai tonight, \"no guarantees\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Text itself tips off: <b>40 years</b> wandering in the Sinai desert</li><li class=\"d0\">Two problems: no food, no water</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>manna and quail</b> (two studies ago)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>water from the rock</b>, God standing on the rock (last time)</li><li class=\"d0\">Then first big visit from <b>Amalekites</b> - come back for several books until <b>2 Samuel</b> (2 Kingdoms)</li><li class=\"d1\">God gave Israel <b>victory, protection</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Getting close to Sinai, where the <b>Torah</b> is officially given to Moses</li></ul>"},{"t":152.12,"lv":2,"h":"18:1 - Jethro hears","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jethro</b>, priest of Midian, Moses' <b>father-in-law</b>, hears all God did for Moses and Israel</li><li class=\"d0\">Met him before: wife <b>Zipporah</b> is his daughter</li><li class=\"d1\">Moses lived in his household the <b>40 years</b> after fleeing Egypt, herding his flocks - the <b>burning bush</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Jethro\" = a <b>title</b> for a Midianite priest, not a given name</li><li class=\"d0\">Heard of <b>Pharaoh's army defeated</b>, plagues, <b>Red Sea</b></li></ul>"},{"t":229.5,"lv":3,"h":"God's name made known to the nations","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God said repeatedly: part of purpose = <b>his name known among the nations</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Three levels</b>: reveals himself to <b>Israel</b> → through Israel to <b>Egyptians</b> → to <b>all nations</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"God cares about Israel and none of these other people\"</li><li class=\"d0\">First sign: <b>Jethro</b>, a nomad in the desert, has heard</li><li class=\"d0\">More signs into <b>Joshua</b> and later: people they meet <b>heard of Egypt</b>; God of Israel has a <b>reputation</b></li></ul>"},{"t":319.53,"lv":2,"h":"18:2-5 - Zipporah and sons arrive","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jethro took <b>Zipporah</b>, sent back, with her <b>two sons</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Gershom</b> - <i>ger</i> = foreigner, sojourner: \"I was a sojourner in a foreign land\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Eliezer</b> = <b>\"God is my help\"</b> - \"God of my father is my help, rescued me from Pharaoh\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Names' origin repeated</li><li class=\"d0\">Came to Moses in the desert, <b>encamped at the mountain of God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Moses hadn't taken them with him; Jethro <b>brings them to him</b></li></ul>"},{"t":408.12,"lv":2,"h":"18:6-11 - Moses and Jethro","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jethro sent word ahead - <b>not unannounced</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Moses went out, <b>bowed down and kissed him</b>, brought him into the tent</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses told <b>all</b> the Lord did to Pharaoh, the hardship on the way, the deliverance</li><li class=\"d1\">Jethro had only heard <b>rumors</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Jethro <b>amazed</b>: \"<b>Blessed be the Lord</b>… Now I know the Lord is great <b>above all the gods</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Lord\" = covering for <b>Yahweh</b></li></ul>"},{"t":475.86,"lv":3,"h":"Yahweh and the Midianites","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Earliest record of <b>Yahweh</b> outside the Bible: <b>Egyptian inscriptions</b> of roughly this period</li><li class=\"d1\">\"god of the <b>Shasu Bedouins</b>\" = <b>Midianites and Edomites</b>, nomads in Sinai</li><li class=\"d0\">Earliest archaeological evidence: God worshiped by people <b>like Jethro</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not a pagan priest - <b>same God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Midianites = mainly <b>Ishmaelites and Edomites</b> → descended from <b>Ishmael and Esau</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Israel's <b>cousins</b>, descended from Abraham, still worshiping <b>Abraham's God</b>, living nomadically as he did</li></ul>"},{"t":588.19,"lv":3,"h":"Egyptian gods: creator vs created","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">At <b>Passover</b>: God judges <b>all the gods of Egypt</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not fictional characters - \"<b>if Batman were real</b>, beating him means tough; he's not, so not impressive\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Egyptian gods <b>existed</b> - actual <b>spirits</b> worshiped and interacted with</li><li class=\"d1\">led Egypt into <b>slavery, oppression, military conquest</b>; society <b>brutal, repressive, violent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not spirits with their <b>best interests</b> at heart - \"other motives\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God judges them → <b>category difference</b> from Yahweh</li><li class=\"d1\">not exists vs doesn't exist</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>creator vs created spirits</b> - came into being at some point</li></ul>"},{"t":723.89,"lv":3,"h":"\"Now I know\" = \"I see\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Sounds like Jethro didn't know before - but already worshiped Yahweh</li><li class=\"d0\">Greek verb for \"know\" built on the verb <b>\"to see\"</b> → \"oh, <b>now I see</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">like a chalkboard demonstration: \"now I get it, plain to me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">What happened <b>demonstrates</b>: Yahweh in a <b>different category</b>, above Egyptian gods</li><li class=\"d0\">Shown: when he came to judge, <b>nothing they could do</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Nile to blood; <b>darkness</b> - <b>Ra</b>, the sun god, could not oppose him</li></ul>"},{"t":816.95,"lv":2,"h":"18:12 - Sacrifice and eating before God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jethro takes <b>whole burnt offerings</b> and sacrifices; <b>Aaron and elders</b> come to <b>eat bread</b> with him <b>before God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Details in <b>Leviticus</b> - \"won't be as long as you think\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Notice: offer sacrifices → <b>ate bread before the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>sacrifices are not about killing things, they're about eating things</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"kill a basket of breadsticks at Olive Garden\" - only analogical killing</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Moloch - baby thrown in? - <b>human sacrifice</b>, <b>cannibalism</b>, <b>blood drinking</b>, sometimes more</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Whole burnt</b> = all burned; other sacrifices: <b>choice portions to God</b>, rest <b>eaten by offerers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sacrifice sits within <b>hospitality</b> = building <b>community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ancient communities included their <b>god(s)</b>; sacrifice, incense, gifts <b>reaffirm or restore</b> relationship - people with each other and with God</li><li class=\"d1\">always involves a <b>shared meal</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1000.27,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: why Jethro leads the offering","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Jethro takes the lead - Moses <b>humble</b>, or Jethro honoring Yahweh?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>No separate priesthood yet</b> - Aaron mentioned, <b>looking forward</b></li><li class=\"d1\">circumcision incident: Moses' authority partly <b>taken and given to Aaron</b> → priestly authority</li><li class=\"d1\">on the <b>cusp</b>, not yet separated</li><li class=\"d0\">Before: <b>head of household = priest</b> - Abraham, Isaac, Jacob</li><li class=\"d1\">Moses still part of <b>Jethro's household</b> - \"essentially his father\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Job</b> sacrificing for his children and their families</li></ul>"},{"t":1095.14,"lv":2,"h":"Priesthood and masculinity","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Torah: integral link between <b>being a man</b> and <b>priesthood</b> - \"relationship of identity\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"what it means to be a man is to be a priest\"</li><li class=\"d1\">female <b>prophets</b>, <b>queens</b> - but female priest = \"<b>square circle</b>\", \"married bachelor\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Why are women excluded from the priesthood?\" - <b>wrong question</b></li><li class=\"d1\">treats priesthood as a thing out there, with being female a disqualifier</li><li class=\"d1\">really: <b>priesthood is what it means to be male</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not stressing it over women priests - \"no levers to make that happen\"</li><li class=\"d1\">stressing it because of the <b>manosphere</b>, men's movement online</li><li class=\"d1\">tells you everything about being a man <b>except being a priest</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1206.31,"lv":3,"h":"The manosphere vs the priest","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: like <b>Liver King</b>? - \"analogically\": <b>spiritual leader of your family</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Online: \"<b>ripped, rich, and sexually promiscuous</b>\" - opposite of priest (\"two Bugattis\" - \"Hot Wheels\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Practical change: <b>disciplining children</b> like <b>confession</b>, not a \"contest of wills\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I told you… I'm the one in charge\" - wouldn't get far with that in the church</li><li class=\"d1\">someone in my family <b>separated from God and family</b> → my job: <b>reconcile</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Blessed are the peacemakers\"</b> - not \"blessed are the victorious\" (Romans)</li><li class=\"d0\">Priesthood still lodged here; later <b>aspects taken from some men</b> for failures</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>calling to all men</b> continues - even unmarried</li><li class=\"d1\">in friend group, workplace: <b>represent God to them and them to God in prayer</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Make peace at work, not gossip\" - yes</li><li class=\"d0\">Priesthood separated out later - good to hold the <b>broad idea</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1442.61,"lv":2,"h":"18:13-16 - Moses judges the people","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Next day Moses sits to <b>judge</b>; people stand <b>morning to evening</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"Simon Cowell\" judging - <b>settling disputes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">camp full of grumblers; \"my goat died, he poisoned it\" / \"he stole my goat\" / unpaid debts</li><li class=\"d1\">Moses makes the call: \"give him back his goat\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sunrise to sunset</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Jethro: \"What is this thing you're doing? Why do you <b>alone</b> sit?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses: people come \"to seek <b>judgment from God</b>\" - I <b>make known the ordinances and laws of God</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1556.81,"lv":2,"h":"Moses' authority from God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God gave Moses the authority → his decision = <b>God's decision</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pre-Christian Judaism: Moses' authority described as <b>binding and loosing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">source of NT terms; Christ gives <b>apostles</b> that authority</li></ul>"},{"t":1634.81,"lv":3,"h":"A text can't have authority by itself","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">End of Torah, <b>commandments</b> laid out - still a thousand questions: \"my situation's a little different\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not possible for a text to exercise authority by itself\" - <b>even the Bible</b></li><li class=\"d1\">someone must <b>read, interpret, apply</b></li><li class=\"d1\">authority must come from <b>God</b> - \"I'm a smart dude… other smart dudes differ\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses, then <b>successors</b> to Jesus' time</li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus on <b>Pharisees</b>: do what they say - \"<b>sit in Moses' seat</b>\"; not what they do (hypocrites)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>apostles</b> given binding and loosing → they decide</li></ul>"},{"t":1763.24,"lv":3,"h":"Paul and the courts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Paul</b> chews out Corinthians for taking disputes to <b>Roman courts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"why go to pagans to resolve disputes with fellow Christians?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Still in the <b>canons</b>: sue a fellow Orthodox → <b>laicized</b> if clergy, <b>excommunicated</b> if not</li></ul>"},{"t":1839.29,"lv":3,"h":"Matthew 18 and the bishop's last word","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Matthew 18</b> - process already existing, reaffirmed</li><li class=\"d1\">talk to the person → bring a couple more → <b>priest</b> → <b>bishop</b></li><li class=\"d1\">bishop's word is it - \"<b>court of last appeal</b>\", not about right/wrong</li><li class=\"d0\">No <b>canon law</b> - <b>canons</b> are tools for the bishop to apply</li><li class=\"d1\">your job: <b>be obedient</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Bishop ignorant, sloppy, vindictive → <b>God takes it up on the day of judgment</b>; same for the priest</li><li class=\"d1\">suffering mistreatment → God resolves; rewards <b>patient suffering</b>, deals with mistreaters</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"they got away with it\" or \"lousy life\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Since the Torah: a <b>text as tool</b> + <b>human authority</b> from God interpreting it</li><li class=\"d0\">Q&amp;A: neighbor calls himself a Christian - would go to <b>pagan court</b>; ideal: his bishop settles it</li><li class=\"d1\">bishop can't excommunicate one <b>not a member</b>; no appeal to other bishops - <b>stops there</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2109.52,"lv":2,"h":"18:17-23 - Jethro's counsel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"The thing you are doing is <b>not good</b>\" - \"great roundabout translation\"</li><li class=\"d0\">You'll <b>wear yourselves out</b>; too much for you alone</li><li class=\"d1\">thousands in camp; can't do anything else → <b>need help</b></li><li class=\"d0\">V.19: \"I will give you counsel, and God will be with you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">your job: <b>stand before God for the people</b>, bring their concerns - <b>mediator</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>teach them</b> God's ordinances, laws, the way to walk</li><li class=\"d1\">represent <b>them to God</b>, <b>God to them</b></li><li class=\"d0\">V.21: select <b>able men</b> who <b>fear God</b>, <b>just</b>, <b>hating covetousness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">rulers of <b>thousands, hundreds, fifties, tens</b></li><li class=\"d1\">judge at all times; <b>great matters</b> to you, <b>small</b> themselves → they <b>relieve and help</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2221.88,"lv":3,"h":"Lesser clergy and Paul's pastoral epistles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"You need some <b>lesser clergy</b>, Moses\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same qualities <b>Paul</b> gives for <b>presbyters and deacons</b> (1 and 2 Timothy, Titus)</li><li class=\"d1\">Timothy and Titus were <b>bishops</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul not making up a \"new religion\" - goes back to the <b>Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Moses = <b>paradigm of a bishop</b>; helpers take small matters so he keeps his <b>primary job</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>19th-century German critics</b>: Timothy/Titus \"can't be Paul - no complicated leadership until 2nd century\"</li><li class=\"d1\">structures around <b>1500 years before Paul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christianity = <b>sect of Judaism</b>, continuing with belief the <b>Messiah had come</b> (a Jewish belief) - <b>no discontinuity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">apostles had same structures; \"didn't start with none\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Sorry, anarchists\" - <b>no culture, society, family</b> ever truly <b>anarchy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">always someone in charge: strongest, elder, smartest manipulator</li><li class=\"d1\">early church = no \"<b>anarcho-communist commune</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2441.84,"lv":2,"h":"18:23-27 - Moses heeds Jethro","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">V.23: do this → God will <b>strengthen you</b>; people go to their place <b>in peace</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Moses <b>heeded</b> his father-in-law, chose <b>able men</b> as heads: thousands, hundreds, fifties, tens</li><li class=\"d1\">judged at all times; <b>hard cases</b> to Moses, small ones themselves</li><li class=\"d0\">Jethro <b>departs</b> to his own land</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses lets Jethro offer sacrifices <b>and</b> takes advice</li><li class=\"d1\">clear on his God-given authority yet <b>accedes to wise counsel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Later Torah: Moses the <b>most humble man on earth</b> - \"traditionally written by Moses, so figure that out\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2531.03,"lv":3,"h":"Not just proof-texting: the liturgical tradition","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Internet: \"Father Stephen's making this up\" - doesn't <b>proof-text fathers</b></li><li class=\"d0\">But reading this as <b>bishops, priests, deacons</b> - this reading is read at the <b>feast of the ecumenical councils</b> about bishops</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not making it up, or plagiarizing - the <b>liturgical tradition</b> of the church\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2581.37,"lv":3,"h":"Acts: the seven deacons","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts</b>: Jerusalem, <b>apostles</b> in charge</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Greek-speaking Jewish widows</b> not fed → complaint</li><li class=\"d1\">apostles: \"we have this job from God\" → appoint <b>first seven deacons</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not shirking, not inventing a structure - <b>back to the Torah</b>: \"here's how Moses handled it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Deacons' qualities <b>suspiciously similar</b> to Exodus 18</li></ul>"}]});
